These four metrics help us understand whether a large-cap mutual fund delivers consistent returns, beats the market, and controls losses. They measure different things, so we use them together in your F2 framework.
Let's use simple examples.
1. 3Y rolling periods beating the Nifty 50 proxy
Meaning: The percentage of historical three-year investment periods in which a fund outperformed the Nifty 50 reference index.
Suppose we examine a fund over 10 years. We calculate its return for successive three-year windows, shifting the start date each month.
For example:
Three-year window	Fund annualised return	Index annualised return	Beat index?
Jan 2017–Jan 2020	13%	11%	Yes
Feb 2017–Feb 2020	12%	12.5%	No
Mar 2017–Mar 2020	14%	10%	Yes
…	…	…	…
If the fund beats the index in 80 out of 100 windows, its rolling beat rate is 80%.
"Beat rate"="Windows beating index" /"Total windows" ×100

How to interpret it:
	80%: the fund beat the index in 8 out of 10 comparable windows, on average.
	50%: it beat the index in half the windows.
	30%: it beat the index in fewer than one-third of the windows.
A higher percentage indicates more consistent historical outperformance, but does not guarantee future results.
What does “proxy” mean? It means a substitute reference index. In our earlier table, it was a Nifty 50 price-return index rather than necessarily the fund's official total-return benchmark. For final F2 scoring, we should use the appropriate TRI benchmark because it includes dividends.
2. Median 3Y rolling return
Meaning: The middle annualised return across all the fund's historical three-year investment windows.
Suppose the fund's rolling three-year returns are:
6%, 9%, 11%, 14%, 25%
The median is 11% — the middle number after sorting the returns.
Why is this useful? A few exceptionally good periods can inflate a fund's average return. The median gives us a better sense of what a typical historical three-year period looked like. 
 
